Riemannian Geometry (Do Carmo & Flaherty Francis, 1992). A smooth manifold M generalizes the notion of surface to higher dimensions. Each point x ∈ M is associated with a tangent space TxM, which is locally Euclidean. On tangent space TxM, the Riemannian metric, gx(·, ·) : TxM× TxM → R, defines an inner product so that geometric notions (like distance, angle, etc.) can be induced. The pair (M, g) is called a Riemannian manifold. For x ∈ M, the exponential map at x, expx(v) : TxM → M, projects the vector v ∈ TxM onto the manifold M, and the logarithmic map, logx(y) : M → TxM, projects the vector y ∈ M back to the tangent space TxM. The Riemannian metric defines a curvature (κ) at each point on the manifold, indicating how the space is curved. There are three canonical types: positively curved Spherical (S) space (κ > 0), negatively curved Hyperbolic (H) space (κ < 0), and flat Euclidean (E) space (κ = 0).
